And you know, 'cause the way it,

00:19:55.980 --> 00:19:57.800
the way I feel about it is,

00:19:57.800 --> 00:20:00.010
is that whatever mood he's in,

00:20:00.010 --> 00:20:01.700
when he is visiting a certain place

00:20:01.700 --> 00:20:03.020
and the timing works out.

00:20:03.020 --> 00:20:05.520
It's just like the old principle of,

00:20:05.520 --> 00:20:09.100
you get married based on
when you meet somebody

00:20:09.100 --> 00:20:10.840
in life circumstances.

00:20:10.840 --> 00:20:14.780
It, the super practical,
like completely unromantic,

00:20:14.780 --> 00:20:15.980
there's no such thing as soulmates,

00:20:15.980 --> 00:20:17.580
which I'm not saying I subscribe to,

00:20:17.580 --> 00:20:19.490
but some people would say marriage is a,

00:20:19.490 --> 00:20:22.500
is just a, is who you are
in love with at the time

00:20:22.500 --> 00:20:24.200
when you could get married, right?

00:20:25.240 --> 00:20:27.000
I don't necessarily believe that,

00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:29.250
but there is an element
of circumstance with who,

00:20:29.250 --> 00:20:30.170
and hopefully you get lucky,

00:20:30.170 --> 00:20:31.250
and you're with the right person,

00:20:31.250 --> 00:20:32.550
I feel that way about my marriage.

00:20:32.550 --> 00:20:34.051
But, when it comes to college,

00:20:34.051 --> 00:20:36.540
it's just like, you think
about how circumstantial.

00:20:36.540 --> 00:20:39.010
And again, we just chose between
Carolina and State, right?

00:20:39.010 --> 00:20:40.620
That was the two only two
schools we applied to,

00:20:40.620 --> 00:20:42.340
got accepted in both.
Yeah.

00:20:42.340 --> 00:20:47.010
And then, like, I remember
taking a tour of UNC.

00:20:47.010 --> 00:20:49.860
And again, if you remember this,

00:20:49.860 --> 00:20:51.897
I was probably talking to you like,

00:20:51.897 --> 00:20:53.190
"This is what, we would do this,

00:20:53.190 --> 00:20:54.240
and this is how."

00:20:54.240 --> 00:20:56.567
I'm living out the next
four years and being like,

00:20:56.567 --> 00:20:57.860
"This is what it's gonna be like."

00:20:57.860 --> 00:20:59.914
And then going to state,
and doing the same thing.

00:20:59.914 --> 00:21:01.630
In the end, it was...

00:21:01.630 --> 00:21:04.700
I do have a hard time
remembering the specifics of it,

00:21:04.700 --> 00:21:06.362
but knowing how different.

00:21:06.362 --> 00:21:08.140
UNC was just the two of us.

00:21:08.140 --> 00:21:10.270
we drove ourselves up there,
I remember that specifically.

00:21:10.270 --> 00:21:11.103
Oh, really?
No parents,

00:21:11.103 --> 00:21:12.330
we just drove up there together

00:21:12.330 --> 00:21:13.303
and walked around.

00:21:14.260 --> 00:21:16.350
And it's a really impressive campus.

00:21:16.350 --> 00:21:18.810
So, it was very enticing.

00:21:18.810 --> 00:21:22.970
But the entire time, like
we were never Carolina fans,

00:21:22.970 --> 00:21:24.820
I was always a State fan.

00:21:24.820 --> 00:21:25.877
And you were a Duke fan.

00:21:25.877 --> 00:21:26.770
I was a Duke fan.

00:21:26.770 --> 00:21:28.610
So, we were from out of town.

00:21:28.610 --> 00:21:31.590
It was like, we felt, we
didn't want anybody to see us.

00:21:31.590 --> 00:21:33.500
Like, not that anybody would...

00:21:33.500 --> 00:21:35.074
Yeah, I, you may have felt that way.

00:21:35.074 --> 00:21:37.127
I didn't have a strong
feelings about that.

00:21:37.127 --> 00:21:38.630
And my brother was already there.

00:21:38.630 --> 00:21:39.463
You know-
Okay, yeah.

00:21:39.463 --> 00:21:40.510
my brother was at school there.

00:21:41.740 --> 00:21:43.277
We ended up going to State
just because it was like,

00:21:43.277 --> 00:21:44.960
"Oh yeah, I'm taken physics,

00:21:44.960 --> 00:21:47.780
and I like Mr. Bryant, who's teaching it."

00:21:47.780 --> 00:21:48.613
You know what I'm saying?

00:21:48.613 --> 00:21:49.510
It was such a...

00:21:49.510 --> 00:21:50.343
Oh yeah.

00:21:50.343 --> 00:21:52.610
But, because I know that
that's how kids make decisions.

00:21:52.610 --> 00:21:55.860
It's, and Locke can get so
passionate about something.

00:21:55.860 --> 00:21:59.500
I'm trying to sort of modulate,

00:21:59.500 --> 00:22:02.120
mitigate, whatever the word is.

00:22:02.120 --> 00:22:04.440
And then, the opportunity

00:22:04.440 --> 00:22:08.570
to go to a basketball
game presented itself.

00:22:08.570 --> 00:22:09.460
Oh, okay.

00:22:09.460 --> 00:22:12.430
And this particular school
that we were looking at,

00:22:12.430 --> 00:22:14.330
Locke gets on his phone the night before.

00:22:14.330 --> 00:22:17.757
And he's like, "Dad,
they're playing Gonzaga."

00:22:19.100 --> 00:22:20.190
Gonzaga-
Pretty good.

00:22:20.190 --> 00:22:22.150
Number two team in the nation,

00:22:22.150 --> 00:22:24.350
with the number one player in the nation.

00:22:24.350 --> 00:22:26.460
It's like a six, it's like
a seven-foot point guard.

00:22:26.460 --> 00:22:27.440
I don't even remember the dude's name,

00:22:27.440 --> 00:22:29.820
but he's like a seven-foot point guard.

00:22:29.820 --> 00:22:32.921
That's like very, it would be
amazing to watch, you know,

00:22:32.921 --> 00:22:33.920
just imagine that.
Be a good game.

00:22:33.920 --> 00:22:35.487
And it might seal the deal for like,

00:22:35.487 --> 00:22:36.857
"Oh, I had such a great experience."

00:22:36.857 --> 00:22:37.804
"I have to go here."

00:22:37.804 --> 00:22:38.637
Well, that's what I was thinking.

00:22:38.637 --> 00:22:39.470
And I was also thinking like,

00:22:39.470 --> 00:22:40.303
you, 'cause this is a, not,

00:22:40.303 --> 00:22:43.470
it's a relatively small
school with like a gym,

00:22:43.470 --> 00:22:45.328
That's like the size
of like Campbell's gym.

00:22:45.328 --> 00:22:46.161
Oh wow.

00:22:46.161 --> 00:22:47.640
And going and seeing like Gonzaga play,

00:22:47.640 --> 00:22:49.120
in a place like that.

00:22:49.120 --> 00:22:52.073
So we get on, we go on
SeatGeek, not a sponsor.

00:22:53.040 --> 00:22:56.390
And we find three, we find
three tickets, not four.

00:22:56.390 --> 00:22:58.010
And Jessie's like, "It's
fine, I don't really care.

00:22:58.010 --> 00:22:59.810
I'll just like, well,
I'll go to a coffee shop,

00:22:59.810 --> 00:23:01.060
hang out while you guys go to the game."

00:23:01.060 --> 00:23:03.313
So, we're like, "Okay", three tickets.

00:23:04.440 --> 00:23:09.440
Last minute, Gonzaga
tickets, 150 bucks a pop.

00:23:09.500 --> 00:23:10.333
Okay.

00:23:10.333 --> 00:23:11.577
So it's like, this is
a, but I'm like this,

00:23:11.577 --> 00:23:12.950
"Hey listen, let's do this

00:23:12.950 --> 00:23:13.950
because this is..."

00:23:13.950 --> 00:23:14.970
What else are you gonna be doing?

00:23:14.970 --> 00:23:16.040
It's a cool experience.

00:23:16.040 --> 00:23:16.897
But I was also thinking,

00:23:16.897 --> 00:23:19.320
"Man, even though they're
definitely gonna get beat

00:23:19.320 --> 00:23:22.140
by Gonzaga." this is going,

00:23:22.140 --> 00:23:24.200
if he sees like other students like there,

00:23:24.200 --> 00:23:25.240
and he has this experience,

00:23:25.240 --> 00:23:27.030
this is, might seal the deal for him.

00:23:27.030 --> 00:23:28.950
So, but that's not
necessarily a good thing.

00:23:28.950 --> 00:23:29.897
I'm worried about it,
but I'm almost so like,

00:23:29.897 --> 00:23:32.040
I kind of wanna see this game.

00:23:32.040 --> 00:23:33.020
Okay.

00:23:33.020 --> 00:23:37.280
So, we get the tickets and then game day,

00:23:37.280 --> 00:23:38.360
which was the next day.

00:23:38.360 --> 00:23:41.030
We go up to the gym, you
know, like half an hour early.

00:23:41.030 --> 00:23:43.200
And the guy like sees that
I pull out the tickets.

00:23:43.200 --> 00:23:44.787
He's like,

00:23:44.787 --> 00:23:48.907
"So we're operating at 25%
capacity today, due to COVID."

00:23:50.417 --> 00:23:51.597
"So, we're gonna scan your tickets."

00:23:51.597 --> 00:23:53.537
"If they scan green, you can come in."

00:23:53.537 --> 00:23:54.910
"If they scan red..."

00:23:54.910 --> 00:23:57.030
Oh wow.
"You can't."

00:23:57.030 --> 00:23:59.730
And he said, "And the
way we're doing that is,

00:23:59.730 --> 00:24:03.720
is that all season ticket
holders are allowed to come in,

00:24:03.720 --> 00:24:08.170
but anyone who bought single game tickets

00:24:08.170 --> 00:24:10.130
and then resold them,

00:24:10.130 --> 00:24:11.880
they've already been
refunded for these tickets.

00:24:11.880 --> 00:24:13.930
And so, it's not necessarily a scam,

00:24:13.930 --> 00:24:15.017
but they should have contacted SeatGeek,

00:24:15.017 --> 00:24:16.750
and taken the tickets off.

00:24:16.750 --> 00:24:17.583
And so he is like,

00:24:17.583 --> 00:24:18.800
"I don't know if yours are season tickets,

00:24:18.800 --> 00:24:21.547
or single, the only way to
find out is to scan it."

00:24:22.780 --> 00:24:24.100
You're like, "Oh my goodness".

00:24:24.100 --> 00:24:25.473
And I'm like, "Oh man,
this could change like..."

00:24:25.473 --> 00:24:27.780
like I'm thinking the,

00:24:27.780 --> 00:24:30.730
I'm thinking on I'm on a dad
level at this point, right?

00:24:30.730 --> 00:24:34.600
So, it's like the green for me,

00:24:34.600 --> 00:24:36.820
represents him being
excited about this school

00:24:36.820 --> 00:24:38.650
and the red I'm like, this is
gonna ruin his, everything.

00:24:38.650 --> 00:24:40.900
He's gonna have this
sour taste in his mouth

00:24:40.900 --> 00:24:42.480
about this school, and
he's not gonna want to go.

00:24:42.480 --> 00:24:47.480
Hmm, and did the guy tell
you that if it was red,

00:24:47.590 --> 00:24:50.470
that it was, you didn't get
your money back, or you did?

00:24:50.470 --> 00:24:54.430
He said, well, he didn't tell me that yet.

00:24:54.430 --> 00:24:55.400
Okay, well go ahead then.

00:24:55.400 --> 00:24:58.233
He scanned them, and they scanned red.

00:24:59.645 --> 00:25:01.640
And I was like, "Oh gosh man, dang,

00:25:01.640 --> 00:25:02.610
we've planned this whole day,

00:25:02.610 --> 00:25:04.690
coming down here to watch this game."

00:25:04.690 --> 00:25:06.760
And like Locke is obviously
really disappointed.

00:25:06.760 --> 00:25:08.410
Shepherds, just kind of chilling.

00:25:09.929 --> 00:25:11.157
At that point, he said,

00:25:11.157 --> 00:25:13.540
"You need to contact SeatGeek." because

00:25:15.552 --> 00:25:16.900
and the person's name,

00:25:16.900 --> 00:25:18.020
I'm not gonna say the name,

00:25:18.020 --> 00:25:19.900
the person's name is on the ticket.

00:25:19.900 --> 00:25:21.910
And so, like the things
that we were saying

00:25:21.910 --> 00:25:24.450
about this person who had
done this to us was very,

00:25:24.450 --> 00:25:26.910
we were, it was a running
joke the rest of the week,

00:25:26.910 --> 00:25:29.700
about how we were going
to hunt this person down.

00:25:29.700 --> 00:25:31.500
Obviously, we didn't have to do that.

00:25:31.500 --> 00:25:33.550
'Cause we contact the
SeatGeek and actually,

00:25:33.550 --> 00:25:35.060
well they're like investigating it.

00:25:35.060 --> 00:25:35.893
But I'm pretty,

00:25:35.893 --> 00:25:38.820
they said that you will get
up to 120% of your money back.

00:25:38.820 --> 00:25:39.890
Well, you might make money off it.

00:25:39.890 --> 00:25:41.650
Yeah, this might be a new way to like make

00:25:41.650 --> 00:25:42.937
some side money.
Okay, there you go.

00:25:42.937 --> 00:25:44.308
Go, not go to basketball games.

00:25:46.440 --> 00:25:48.120
But I think-
So, you had to pivot.

00:25:48.120 --> 00:25:49.290
It happened with multiple people,

00:25:49.290 --> 00:25:50.570
I think we'll get our money back.

00:25:50.570 --> 00:25:52.130
But, it was interesting 'cause like,

00:25:52.130 --> 00:25:54.080
Locke was definitely disappointed.

00:25:54.080 --> 00:25:57.780
And then, but he's kind
of been very interested

00:25:57.780 --> 00:26:00.720
in this place and it kind of was a,

00:26:00.720 --> 00:26:02.850
threw a little bit of cold water on him.

00:26:02.850 --> 00:26:05.880
But then, I felt like it was,

00:26:05.880 --> 00:26:08.300
it kind of centered him a little bit.

00:26:08.300 --> 00:26:11.460
And he's still very into
the possibility of going,

00:26:11.460 --> 00:26:13.770
but he was very excited
about the next place.

00:26:13.770 --> 00:26:16.160
And he's very excited about
places that we're gonna see.

00:26:16.160 --> 00:26:17.430
He's gonna hear back from soon,

00:26:17.430 --> 00:26:20.200
but places we might see in the spring.

00:26:20.200 --> 00:26:23.490
And so, I think it actually
was a blessing in disguise,

00:26:23.490 --> 00:26:25.610
because if we had gotten the green check.

00:26:25.610 --> 00:26:30.610
Yeah, it might falsely weigh
the scales, tip the scales.

00:26:32.930 --> 00:26:34.450
But one of the things that I found myself

00:26:34.450 --> 00:26:35.730
thinking, and I wonder if this is-

00:26:35.730 --> 00:26:37.602
You might really just
want to go to Gonzaga,

00:26:37.602 --> 00:26:38.435
is really what-

00:26:38.435 --> 00:26:39.268
Yeah, right.
You know?

00:26:39.268 --> 00:26:41.800
If this is the case with you,

00:26:41.800 --> 00:26:44.750
not just, I mean even
just like helping Lily

00:26:44.750 --> 00:26:47.513
move into college, and like
being on a college campus.

00:26:48.970 --> 00:26:53.020
We were, Jessie and I were
trying to explain to the kids

00:26:53.020 --> 00:26:56.480
how different our college experience was.

00:26:56.480 --> 00:26:59.220
There's all these like groups
of people doing things,

00:26:59.220 --> 00:27:02.710
and like clubs, and it, activities.

00:27:02.710 --> 00:27:04.340
There's so much to do in college.

00:27:04.340 --> 00:27:05.173
And I was like.
Yeah.

00:27:05.173 --> 00:27:06.960
I don't think you guys understood.

00:27:06.960 --> 00:27:08.600
We did two things.

00:27:08.600 --> 00:27:11.170
The first thing we did
is we went to class,

00:27:11.170 --> 00:27:12.890
and the second thing we did is,

00:27:12.890 --> 00:27:14.800
everything with Campus Crusade.

00:27:14.800 --> 00:27:17.800
Yeah.
We did not do anything else.

00:27:17.800 --> 00:27:22.800
Our entire social life, every
single activity that we had

00:27:23.240 --> 00:27:25.220
was-
Yeah.

00:27:25.220 --> 00:27:26.620
Just, it was our campus ministry

00:27:26.620 --> 00:27:27.970
that we were involved with.

00:27:27.970 --> 00:27:30.810
Which, you know, again,

00:27:30.810 --> 00:27:32.570
I don't have any regrets about where-

00:27:32.570 --> 00:27:33.403
There were a lot of people involved.

00:27:33.403 --> 00:27:34.420
All this lead.

00:27:34.420 --> 00:27:37.320
So, it was like from a social standpoint.

00:27:37.320 --> 00:27:38.210
Oh it.
You know.

00:27:38.210 --> 00:27:39.920
We made a lot of great connections,

00:27:39.920 --> 00:27:41.193
and we had a lot of great relationships.

00:27:41.193 --> 00:27:43.810
I don't regret any of that,

00:27:43.810 --> 00:27:45.860
but it was just so interesting how,

00:27:45.860 --> 00:27:50.860
going in with such a singular
worldview that had already,

00:27:51.190 --> 00:27:54.424
you know, solidified and
really hadn't solidified,

00:27:54.424 --> 00:27:55.257
but it felt like it had solidified.

00:27:56.600 --> 00:27:57.433
Yeah.

00:27:57.433 --> 00:27:59.150
It just, I was like, "Man."
that's one thing that

00:27:59.150 --> 00:28:00.647
I see when I'm on a college campus.

00:28:00.647 --> 00:28:02.430
"Just like there's so much to like learn,

00:28:02.430 --> 00:28:04.360
and experience, and do."

00:28:04.360 --> 00:28:06.423
That I just completely
sealed myself off from,

00:28:06.423 --> 00:28:08.690
because of my worldview at the time.

00:28:08.690 --> 00:28:13.070
Well, I mean Lily's experience so far

00:28:13.070 --> 00:28:14.700
has been it's, you know,

00:28:14.700 --> 00:28:16.880
the opportunities have
been pretty limited.

00:28:16.880 --> 00:28:20.190
The majority of her classes,
she's on a quarter schedule.

00:28:20.190 --> 00:28:22.720
So, it's like, and she's
like three out of her,

00:28:22.720 --> 00:28:25.800
four classes were online.

00:28:25.800 --> 00:28:28.120
Even though, she's was like
taking 'em from her bed,

00:28:28.120 --> 00:28:29.220
and from her desk-
Yeah.

00:28:29.220 --> 00:28:34.220
In her dorm room, and
only one was in person.

00:28:34.450 --> 00:28:37.530
And now, this year,

00:28:37.530 --> 00:28:40.160
things have been delayed
getting going again,

00:28:40.160 --> 00:28:42.393
everything's been online,

00:28:43.440 --> 00:28:45.160
at least temporarily.

00:28:45.160 --> 00:28:47.050
You know, as they're monitoring things,

00:28:47.050 --> 00:28:50.230
but she's, I mean, she is on campus.

00:28:50.230 --> 00:28:53.330
And so, I mean, it was like,

00:28:53.330 --> 00:28:57.250
man, if classes continue
to only be virtual,

00:28:57.250 --> 00:29:00.020
it's like, should we
rethink this whole thing?

00:29:00.020 --> 00:29:01.590
You know, it's like you
start to think those things

00:29:01.590 --> 00:29:03.538
because her experience is-

00:29:03.538 --> 00:29:04.380
'Cause it's not any cheaper.

00:29:04.380 --> 00:29:06.490
She got a, no, she's got her room mates.

00:29:06.490 --> 00:29:08.030
She's met a few people.

00:29:08.030 --> 00:29:10.350
She's doing, you know, she's,

00:29:10.350 --> 00:29:14.420
she got involved, she
joined a garage band.

00:29:14.420 --> 00:29:15.253
That's cool.

00:29:15.253 --> 00:29:17.017
Which I was real, you know,
I'm like, "This is cool".

00:29:17.017 --> 00:29:19.370
"This is another, this is a,

00:29:19.370 --> 00:29:22.320
this is another experience that
you, that you haven't had."

00:29:23.450 --> 00:29:25.510
That was so formative for us,
Right.

00:29:25.510 --> 00:29:27.090
More in high school, than college for us.

00:29:27.090 --> 00:29:29.800
But, I'd still get this sense that like,

00:29:29.800 --> 00:29:33.090
you know, clubs really
aren't kicking into gear

00:29:33.090 --> 00:29:35.720
and the amount of camp,

00:29:35.720 --> 00:29:39.840
the campus life is still
very stifled by COVID.

00:29:39.840 --> 00:29:44.470
So, it's, I'm frustrated on her behalf,

00:29:44.470 --> 00:29:46.420
that, you know, her first year
Yeah.

00:29:46.420 --> 00:29:51.080
Is kind of a, it's kind of
a half college experience.

00:29:51.080 --> 00:29:53.847
But at this point we're like,
"It's still going good."

00:29:53.847 --> 00:29:57.210
"And it's definitely better than nothing."

00:29:57.210 --> 00:29:58.790
And-
Well, that was...

00:29:58.790 --> 00:30:00.620
She's trying to make the most of it.

00:30:00.620 --> 00:30:01.920
And we're just trying to encourage her.

00:30:01.920 --> 00:30:02.827
It's it's harder to say,

00:30:02.827 --> 00:30:06.760
"Well, go out and get involved
in this group or, you know,

00:30:06.760 --> 00:30:11.760
expand your potential, your
pool of friends." you know?

00:30:12.720 --> 00:30:14.610
So, it's not just whoever
you were assigned with,

00:30:14.610 --> 00:30:16.030
it's who you're with.

00:30:16.030 --> 00:30:17.820
But you know, have some autonomy,

00:30:17.820 --> 00:30:19.690
in being able to like meet people,

00:30:19.690 --> 00:30:21.010
get involved in different circles,

00:30:21.010 --> 00:30:23.090
so that you can start
making some decisions

00:30:23.090 --> 00:30:24.650
about the type of people

00:30:24.650 --> 00:30:26.040
you want to associate yourself with.

00:30:26.040 --> 00:30:30.083
I think as a parent, I get
concerned when it's like,

00:30:31.330 --> 00:30:34.200
this is who you're assigned to live with.

00:30:34.200 --> 00:30:38.830
And, you're kind of limited
beyond that, you know?

00:30:38.830 --> 00:30:42.220
So that's an, I'm excited
about the band thing,

00:30:42.220 --> 00:30:44.103
just because of what I said.

00:30:44.103 --> 00:30:45.340
That was formative for us,

00:30:45.340 --> 00:30:48.453
but also just that it's, it represents a,

00:30:49.580 --> 00:30:51.657
an overlapping but different
friend group, you know.

00:30:51.657 --> 00:30:54.460
And you, so you think about
Locke going off next year,

00:30:54.460 --> 00:30:57.120
hopefully things will have gotten,

00:30:57.120 --> 00:30:59.410
you know, gotten to a point where it can

00:30:59.410 --> 00:31:01.790
be a dynamic experience,

00:31:01.790 --> 00:31:05.520
and it be for Lily and then
for Lincoln too, eventually.

00:31:05.520 --> 00:31:07.920
Well, 'cause when you walk
around a college campus,

00:31:07.920 --> 00:31:11.920
and you see the buildings,

00:31:11.920 --> 00:31:13.870
and you see the residence halls,

00:31:13.870 --> 00:31:15.833
and you see the gathering areas,

00:31:18.220 --> 00:31:19.270
that actually hit me.

00:31:19.270 --> 00:31:23.940
It was like, "Man, this
place is designed to be at,

00:31:23.940 --> 00:31:27.930
it's not designed to be at home,

00:31:27.930 --> 00:31:30.170
or in you taking online classes."

00:31:30.170 --> 00:31:31.010
Yeah.

00:31:31.010 --> 00:31:33.480
You know, that's the wonderful thing about

00:31:33.480 --> 00:31:35.470
a classic university experience.

00:31:35.470 --> 00:31:39.670
Which let me just say,
has become, first of all,

00:31:39.670 --> 00:31:43.433
has always been, and is becoming
more and more a privilege.

00:31:44.330 --> 00:31:47.310
Right, but also I think
it's becoming less,

00:31:47.310 --> 00:31:50.170
and less of a necessity.

00:31:50.170 --> 00:31:54.160
Because for a lot of people,
if you don't have a way,

00:31:54.160 --> 00:31:55.570
if you're not getting a scholarship,

00:31:55.570 --> 00:31:57.590
if you're not finding a way to pay for it.

00:31:57.590 --> 00:32:00.070
Whether it's your family,
or your scholarship,

00:32:00.070 --> 00:32:02.910
or you, you're working
through college or whatever.

00:32:02.910 --> 00:32:03.960
Like for most people,

00:32:05.050 --> 00:32:07.710
it's gonna be taking on a bunch of debt,

00:32:07.710 --> 00:32:11.380
and not necessarily having
a job to pay that debt down.

00:32:11.380 --> 00:32:12.980
So, I do think that I,

00:32:12.980 --> 00:32:15.690
it, I also had this
feeling as I walked around

00:32:15.690 --> 00:32:17.360
and looked at all these buildings that

00:32:17.360 --> 00:32:19.170
kids were just beginning to enjoy.

00:32:19.170 --> 00:32:20.730
But for the past two years,

00:32:20.730 --> 00:32:24.380
they've just kind of been
sitting there in a lot of ways.

00:32:24.380 --> 00:32:25.213
This college campus,

00:32:25.213 --> 00:32:26.243
that's not being taken advantage of,

00:32:26.243 --> 00:32:27.880
it's being paid for by a bunch of kids

00:32:27.880 --> 00:32:29.763
who are at home on their laptops.

00:32:31.720 --> 00:32:32.867
But, I was just thinking about like,

00:32:32.867 --> 00:32:34.463
"Yeah, this is not,

00:32:36.400 --> 00:32:39.000
you know, this isn't for everybody."

00:32:39.000 --> 00:32:41.360
and it's gonna become, it has a,

00:32:41.360 --> 00:32:43.120
you sort of feel like you're
walking through something

00:32:43.120 --> 00:32:46.430
that's just not a lasting way
of doing higher education.

00:32:46.430 --> 00:32:47.263
Hmm.

00:32:47.263 --> 00:32:48.771
And so like, take advantage
of it while you can

00:32:48.771 --> 00:32:50.990
kinda feel because, there's, I mean,

00:32:50.990 --> 00:32:54.050
you have to think that all these people

00:32:54.050 --> 00:32:58.100
taking online classes
has, well first of all,

00:32:58.100 --> 00:32:59.060
I think we can all agree.

00:32:59.060 --> 00:33:00.890
They're not as good as in-person classes,

00:33:00.890 --> 00:33:04.710
and we don't want that to
be the new normal, but.

00:33:04.710 --> 00:33:07.300
No good God, I look over Lily's shoulder

00:33:07.300 --> 00:33:10.410
when she, you know, she
didn't go back as early

00:33:10.410 --> 00:33:11.867
as we planned because she was like,

00:33:11.867 --> 00:33:13.150
"I'll just stay another week,

00:33:13.150 --> 00:33:15.317
'cause I'ma take all my classes online."

00:33:16.430 --> 00:33:17.690
See my high school friends,

00:33:17.690 --> 00:33:19.150
that I'm still been hanging out with,

00:33:19.150 --> 00:33:21.580
have some quality time
with them before I go back.

00:33:21.580 --> 00:33:23.370
I'm looking over her shoulder,

00:33:23.370 --> 00:33:25.690
at like a lecture, she's
like eating breakfast

00:33:25.690 --> 00:33:27.620
and like walking around the
house with it on her phone

00:33:27.620 --> 00:33:29.612
and with her, I was like,

00:33:29.612 --> 00:33:31.590
"Take your earbuds out,

00:33:31.590 --> 00:33:33.030
let me listen to this."
I wanna hear this.

00:33:33.030 --> 00:33:37.340
And it was just some guy just, I mean,

00:33:37.340 --> 00:33:39.060
just drawning on and on, and on,

00:33:39.060 --> 00:33:42.760
about just like speaking extemporaneously

00:33:42.760 --> 00:33:44.820
and like trying to find his words.

00:33:44.820 --> 00:33:46.780
he might be doing that
in class as well, but.

00:33:46.780 --> 00:33:48.430
Yeah, but when you're there in the room,

00:33:48.430 --> 00:33:50.020
and there's other people
and you're like, okay...

00:33:50.020 --> 00:33:51.190
It's, yeah, it's not as hard.

00:33:51.190 --> 00:33:52.720
It can't, I mean that,

00:33:52.720 --> 00:33:55.550
it's just so hard when it's just a screen.

00:33:55.550 --> 00:33:56.790
Well, and getting out of your,

00:33:56.790 --> 00:33:58.803
I think for me, part of it is,

00:33:59.830 --> 00:34:00.950
get getting out of your roots,

00:34:00.950 --> 00:34:01.920
getting out of your house.

00:34:01.920 --> 00:34:03.860
And literally having to
like put a backpack on,

00:34:03.860 --> 00:34:04.930
and walk to a place,

00:34:04.930 --> 00:34:07.380
and go into an institution
of higher learning,

00:34:07.380 --> 00:34:09.860
with other people who are
there for the same purpose.

00:34:09.860 --> 00:34:10.693
Yeah.

00:34:10.693 --> 00:34:14.016
There's a lot of psychology in that.

00:34:14.016 --> 00:34:14.849
Right.
That is helpful

00:34:14.849 --> 00:34:18.480
to most people, but
there's no doubt that this,

00:34:18.480 --> 00:34:21.830
that COVID has accelerated
the shift, right?

00:34:21.830 --> 00:34:24.280
'Cause people are like, "Whoa,
whoa, whoa, I'm paying,"

00:34:24.280 --> 00:34:27.710
you know, in some cases
like "$50,000 a year

00:34:27.710 --> 00:34:32.710
for like a private school for
like University of Phoenix."

00:34:34.280 --> 00:34:35.160
You know what I'm saying?

00:34:35.160 --> 00:34:35.993
Like nothing
I mean.

00:34:35.993 --> 00:34:37.850
And nothing against people
who have online degrees.

00:34:37.850 --> 00:34:41.050
I'm just saying that
the difference in value,

00:34:41.050 --> 00:34:42.450
but to the value proposition,

00:34:42.450 --> 00:34:44.990
between these two things
over the past couple years

00:34:44.990 --> 00:34:47.220
has really, really gone down.

00:34:47.220 --> 00:34:49.320
The difference in the
value proposition because,

00:34:49.320 --> 00:34:50.290
you're not really, I mean,

00:34:50.290 --> 00:34:51.600
you're not getting that much different

00:34:51.600 --> 00:34:54.550
than what you would've
gotten from something online,

00:34:54.550 --> 00:34:57.000
but you're still paying so much for it.

00:34:57.000 --> 00:35:00.710
And in the meantime, the
prices just continue to go up.

00:35:00.710 --> 00:35:03.640
I mean, listen, as a person
who's potentially considering

00:35:03.640 --> 00:35:07.760
a run for Governor, I gotta
say, this is a real problem.

00:35:07.760 --> 00:35:09.113
This is a real problem.

00:35:10.090 --> 00:35:11.630
We gotta figure this out.

00:35:11.630 --> 00:35:15.180
Well, I mean, I wanted
to ask at this point,

00:35:15.180 --> 00:35:17.970
do you have a sense of like,

00:35:17.970 --> 00:35:20.340
and this is kind of switching gears

00:35:20.340 --> 00:35:21.853
but still with parenting,

00:35:22.720 --> 00:35:26.020
of the stakes associated
with this decision?

00:35:26.020 --> 00:35:28.600
'Cause you can really,
I mean, I think for me,

00:35:28.600 --> 00:35:29.537
I could really overthink it,

00:35:29.537 --> 00:35:31.070
and kind of wig out over it.

00:35:31.070 --> 00:35:33.280
It's like, you know,
if Locke gets accepted

00:35:33.280 --> 00:35:34.510
at multiple places,

00:35:34.510 --> 00:35:37.550
he's making this decision
that then he's keenly aware

00:35:37.550 --> 00:35:40.560
because he's future-minded,

00:35:40.560 --> 00:35:42.610
that it's like this leads
to this, leads to this,

00:35:42.610 --> 00:35:44.400
leads to this.

00:35:44.400 --> 00:35:46.330
And there's a fork in the road that,

00:35:46.330 --> 00:35:48.860
that leads to that, leads
to that, leads to that.

00:35:48.860 --> 00:35:50.770
And never the two shall meet.

00:35:50.770 --> 00:35:52.660
It's like you can, maybe
you can overthink it,

00:35:52.660 --> 00:35:56.790
over dramatize it, and the
butterfly effect of it,

00:35:56.790 --> 00:35:57.950
so to speak.

00:35:57.950 --> 00:36:00.510
But it's, you can really
get in your head about it,

00:36:00.510 --> 00:36:03.390
'cause it's a huge milestone decision.

00:36:03.390 --> 00:36:04.930
And you're not quite there yet.

00:36:04.930 --> 00:36:05.763
So, maybe it's not...

00:36:05.763 --> 00:36:07.940
But the, but I, no I think about this.

00:36:07.940 --> 00:36:09.810
There's a few things that are helpful,

00:36:09.810 --> 00:36:11.920
in this particular regard.

00:36:11.920 --> 00:36:15.280
The first thing is Locke's nature, right?

00:36:15.280 --> 00:36:17.720
So, even if I had a specific plan

00:36:17.720 --> 00:36:19.410
that I thought was best for him,

00:36:19.410 --> 00:36:21.900
I am incapable and believe that,

00:36:21.900 --> 00:36:24.990
anyone would be incapable
of prescribing that to him

00:36:24.990 --> 00:36:26.850
in a way that would be effective.

00:36:26.850 --> 00:36:29.870
So, it would be kind of a hopeless,

00:36:29.870 --> 00:36:31.700
it'll be a lost cause to try to tell him

00:36:31.700 --> 00:36:33.830
where he needs to go, or
tell him what's best for him.

00:36:33.830 --> 00:36:36.300
He is gonna learn things his own way.

00:36:36.300 --> 00:36:39.080
I can provide advice and
I can provide information,

00:36:39.080 --> 00:36:42.770
but it's, he's so on his own

00:36:42.770 --> 00:36:46.430
that I have such little
influence in that area,

00:36:46.430 --> 00:36:48.260
that it feels like there's less pressure.

00:36:48.260 --> 00:36:50.940
The second thing is just a general,

00:36:50.940 --> 00:36:52.750
I think it's very healthy,

00:36:52.750 --> 00:36:54.690
the thing that we've
been talking about a lot,

00:36:54.690 --> 00:36:57.520
in, Jessie and I have
been talking a lot about,

00:36:57.520 --> 00:37:00.330
is loving detachment.

00:37:00.330 --> 00:37:03.800
You know, the principle of supporting-

00:37:03.800 --> 00:37:08.800
So, loving detachment is not
a verb and noun, it's a term.

00:37:09.180 --> 00:37:10.260
Yeah.
You don't enjoy

00:37:10.260 --> 00:37:13.970
being detached, it's practicing
something called loving.

00:37:13.970 --> 00:37:15.490
It is detachment, that is loving.

00:37:15.490 --> 00:37:16.360
Okay, what is that?

00:37:16.360 --> 00:37:18.533
So, it is,

00:37:19.870 --> 00:37:20.703
I mean, first of all,

00:37:20.703 --> 00:37:24.760
your motive is you're
obviously you love your kid,

00:37:24.760 --> 00:37:27.130
and you are invested in their future.

00:37:27.130 --> 00:37:29.340
You're helping them, you're
providing them with resources.

00:37:29.340 --> 00:37:30.173
You're doing all the stuff

00:37:30.173 --> 00:37:32.160
that you have control over, right.

00:37:32.160 --> 00:37:34.570
But what you do not have control over,

00:37:34.570 --> 00:37:36.260
is the decisions that they make.

00:37:36.260 --> 00:37:38.593
You have control over
how they're supported,

00:37:40.430 --> 00:37:42.530
and you can have control
over like whether or not

00:37:42.530 --> 00:37:45.763
you take them to the school
to look at it, right.

00:37:46.600 --> 00:37:48.800
But you don't have control
over what they choose.

00:37:48.800 --> 00:37:50.280
And if you think you
have control over that,

00:37:50.280 --> 00:37:51.750
you are sorely mistaken.

00:37:51.750 --> 00:37:53.810
And if you exert control over it,

00:37:53.810 --> 00:37:54.643
then that's-

00:37:54.643 --> 00:37:55.870
It will backfire.

00:37:55.870 --> 00:37:57.140
That could be trouble.

00:37:57.140 --> 00:37:59.810
And so, this isn't just
to do with college.

00:37:59.810 --> 00:38:02.550
This is a principle that
we have sort of been,

00:38:02.550 --> 00:38:06.180
really talking about a lot
over the past couple of years.

00:38:06.180 --> 00:38:07.840
Jessie and I have been talking about.

00:38:07.840 --> 00:38:09.480
Just raising kids in general,

00:38:09.480 --> 00:38:11.230
but especially raising teenagers,

00:38:11.230 --> 00:38:12.970
as you begin to get to that threshold

00:38:12.970 --> 00:38:14.930
where they're beginning
to become their own people

00:38:14.930 --> 00:38:17.640
and they are making their
own decisions about things.

00:38:17.640 --> 00:38:19.640
And if, you know, kids are different.

00:38:19.640 --> 00:38:22.280
Like I've said, multiple times,

00:38:22.280 --> 00:38:24.530
Locke is on the high end of the spectrum

00:38:24.530 --> 00:38:27.090
when it comes to autonomy
and self-motivation,

00:38:27.090 --> 00:38:28.790
and just like his will, right.

00:38:28.790 --> 00:38:31.050
And so you have to,

00:38:31.050 --> 00:38:33.930
this is the only approach
that you cannot dominate him.

00:38:33.930 --> 00:38:35.370
Right, he's in Enneagram eight.

00:38:35.370 --> 00:38:38.460
And their greatest fear,
is being dominated.

00:38:38.460 --> 00:38:40.130
That is the fear of the eight, right?

00:38:40.130 --> 00:38:41.967
And so, that's why
sometimes you could be like,

00:38:41.967 --> 00:38:44.570
"Listen to this book on
tape." or "Let's try this."

00:38:44.570 --> 00:38:46.740
or "Let's do that" or "Let's
go eat at this place."

00:38:46.740 --> 00:38:48.420
And he's like, "No, let's not do that."

00:38:48.420 --> 00:38:50.037
It's in his nature to be like,

00:38:50.037 --> 00:38:53.360
"Oh, I'm not about to be
dominated by you, am I?" right.

00:38:53.360 --> 00:38:54.193
Okay.

00:38:54.193 --> 00:38:56.870
And so, it leads to people
who tend to be do-ers,

00:38:56.870 --> 00:38:59.490
and self-motivated people who go out,

00:38:59.490 --> 00:39:03.003
and make shit happen for themselves a lot.

00:39:04.610 --> 00:39:06.060
But, it also leads to people

00:39:06.060 --> 00:39:07.940
who are gonna learn things their way,

00:39:07.940 --> 00:39:10.210
and maybe learn things
the hard way sometimes.

00:39:10.210 --> 00:39:11.173
So-
Yeah.

00:39:11.173 --> 00:39:14.740
It's, this is not easy,

00:39:14.740 --> 00:39:16.590
and yet, in loving detachment,

00:39:16.590 --> 00:39:18.150
so, you have to,

00:39:18.150 --> 00:39:21.100
you gotta still bring the
love to the situation.

00:39:21.100 --> 00:39:25.920
But yeah, I, so to answer
your question specifically.

00:39:25.920 --> 00:39:28.920
100%, I am aware of this dynamic,

00:39:28.920 --> 00:39:32.763
which is the slightest little thing,

00:39:33.950 --> 00:39:37.060
the butterfly flapping its wings,

00:39:37.060 --> 00:39:39.333
could have this catastrophic,

00:39:40.260 --> 00:39:42.030
or very monumental effect, right.

00:39:42.030 --> 00:39:44.650
And so, where he decides to go to school

00:39:44.650 --> 00:39:46.953
is going to determine his life path.

00:39:48.540 --> 00:39:52.170
But, this is the third piece
of the puzzle for Locke,

00:39:52.170 --> 00:39:56.350
is that he, I mean, he's
probably gonna transfer

00:39:56.350 --> 00:39:58.460
no matter where he goes,
you know what I'm saying?

00:39:58.460 --> 00:40:01.710
Like he's so like, he's
always looking to mix it up

00:40:01.710 --> 00:40:05.970
and try something new that I
just don't even have the...

00:40:05.970 --> 00:40:08.620
Like, he's, I mean, maybe
he'll stay at the same place,

00:40:08.620 --> 00:40:10.310
but he's gonna want to be something else.

00:40:10.310 --> 00:40:11.330
He's gonna do something else.

00:40:11.330 --> 00:40:12.850
He's going to try something else.

00:40:12.850 --> 00:40:14.670
He's gonna go to a different place.

00:40:14.670 --> 00:40:16.160
And so to me, this is just like,

00:40:16.160 --> 00:40:17.510
this is nature of him

00:40:17.510 --> 00:40:19.990
and he's going to do a
bunch of different things

00:40:19.990 --> 00:40:22.570
and I wanted to help him make
the best decision possible,

00:40:22.570 --> 00:40:25.813
but he's probably gonna change
his mind anyway, you know.

00:40:25.813 --> 00:40:29.770
It's kind of, it's akin to our,

00:40:29.770 --> 00:40:32.560
my experience with Lily in that like,

00:40:32.560 --> 00:40:37.100
I really built up the rite
of passage of her leaving.

00:40:37.100 --> 00:40:39.150
I'm mean you can go back and
listen to those episodes.

00:40:39.150 --> 00:40:41.450
And like, there's a, like in that,

00:40:41.450 --> 00:40:43.960
when I was in that zone of sending her off

00:40:43.960 --> 00:40:46.704
and like dealing with it.

00:40:46.704 --> 00:40:51.530
I said that I dramatized it to
fully embrace the experience,

00:40:51.530 --> 00:40:55.490
but it was, you know, it
was, okay, she's going off.

00:40:55.490 --> 00:40:58.210
And she's, our relationship is different,

00:40:58.210 --> 00:41:00.710
and she's gonna come
home a different person,

00:41:00.710 --> 00:41:04.510
and she's not, she doesn't
live with us anymore.

00:41:04.510 --> 00:41:06.510
And low and behold, you know,

00:41:06.510 --> 00:41:09.120
after last Thanksgiving and Christmas,

00:41:09.120 --> 00:41:13.720
and then like the pandemic situation

00:41:13.720 --> 00:41:15.540
and virtual learning, it's like,

00:41:15.540 --> 00:41:17.300
she's still home way too much.

00:41:17.300 --> 00:41:19.150
You know, like man, it's like,

00:41:19.150 --> 00:41:21.490
we got over that really quick.

00:41:21.490 --> 00:41:23.780
You know, it's like,
"Get on back to college",

00:41:23.780 --> 00:41:27.070
you know, kind of, kind
of a message it's like.

00:41:27.070 --> 00:41:30.833
Yeah, so it's as big of a
decision that it, as it is,

00:41:32.770 --> 00:41:35.400
you know, there's always room to pivot

00:41:35.400 --> 00:41:37.690
and you're kind of always looking for,

00:41:37.690 --> 00:41:42.690
Okay, do I need to, is there a
change on the horizon or not?

00:41:43.740 --> 00:41:46.023
Yeah, and you know, this is-

00:41:46.023 --> 00:41:47.130
Takes a little of pressure off.

00:41:47.130 --> 00:41:50.480
This is really something that I,

00:41:50.480 --> 00:41:54.840
it wasn't even on my radar
when my kids were little,

00:41:54.840 --> 00:41:57.300
and I don't necessarily
think he would be on my radar

00:41:57.300 --> 00:41:58.680
if I wasn't in therapy.

00:41:58.680 --> 00:42:01.230
I mean, I'm, there's other
ways to get to this but,

00:42:03.020 --> 00:42:07.650
just beginning to recognize
how much of what we want

00:42:07.650 --> 00:42:11.090
for our kids is just about us, you know.

00:42:11.090 --> 00:42:13.283
And like we say that it's,

00:42:13.283 --> 00:42:16.320
we say that it's for their best interest.

00:42:16.320 --> 00:42:18.640
And I do believe that a big part of it is.

00:42:18.640 --> 00:42:22.313
I think we all love our
children, genuinely.

00:42:23.460 --> 00:42:26.870
But there, but mixed into that love,

00:42:26.870 --> 00:42:31.870
is a lot of how this
reflects on you, you know?

00:42:32.570 --> 00:42:35.110
And so, like what my kid ends up doing,

00:42:35.110 --> 00:42:36.640
and who they end up being.

00:42:36.640 --> 00:42:38.640
And like, are they gonna be like something

00:42:38.640 --> 00:42:39.473
I can be proud of?

00:42:39.473 --> 00:42:42.400
Are they gonna be someone
that I'm gonna be ashamed of?

00:42:42.400 --> 00:42:44.803
And it that's all about you,
am I gonna be proud of them?

00:42:44.803 --> 00:42:46.340
Am I gonna be ashamed of them?

00:42:46.340 --> 00:42:47.310
Am I gonna need to help them?

00:42:47.310 --> 00:42:48.850
Am I gonna, you know what I'm saying?

00:42:48.850 --> 00:42:53.600
Like, and there's a lot
of that, that I have to,

00:42:53.600 --> 00:42:56.610
that's, it's very difficult
thing for me to just, again,

00:42:56.610 --> 00:43:01.610
that's part of the detachment
is detaching my self-worth,

00:43:04.800 --> 00:43:06.700
to, with my kids' self-worth,

00:43:06.700 --> 00:43:10.780
or my self-worth with my kid's,
you know, accomplishments,

00:43:10.780 --> 00:43:13.920
reputation whatever,
whatever it is, right.

00:43:13.920 --> 00:43:15.220
And kids can feel that too.

00:43:15.220 --> 00:43:17.070
I mean, this is, the story as old as time,

00:43:17.070 --> 00:43:19.870
that kids feel the expectations
of their parents, right.

00:43:20.830 --> 00:43:25.830
But, that's been a helpful
thing to just be like,

00:43:26.860 --> 00:43:30.700
man, okay, what ends up
happening with our kids?

00:43:30.700 --> 00:43:33.320
Where they end up, the career
they ended up choosing,

00:43:33.320 --> 00:43:36.020
the person they end up, you know,

00:43:36.020 --> 00:43:37.730
getting into a relationship with.

00:43:37.730 --> 00:43:39.450
Whether or not, they get married or not,

00:43:39.450 --> 00:43:41.240
like all these things.

00:43:41.240 --> 00:43:42.980
And whether we like it or not,

00:43:42.980 --> 00:43:46.400
we've got sort of these
ingrained scripts in our minds,

00:43:46.400 --> 00:43:47.710
just given where we're from.

00:43:47.710 --> 00:43:50.920
And even though we've kinda laid aside

00:43:50.920 --> 00:43:55.210
a very traditional way
of thinking about things.

00:43:55.210 --> 00:43:57.890
When you look at lives that we've lived,

